Exactly—if “plot armor” is the metaphor, faith is the operational version of it.

Not “nothing bad can happen to me.” That version becomes recklessness. More like: “I do not need certainty about the ending in order to act meaningfully in the present.”

Faith converts an unresolved possibility into behavior. You still perceive danger, uncertainty, ghosts in the dark, whatever—but you refuse to let the absence of proof paralyze you. You act as though the next foothold will exist when you put your foot down.

And there’s a useful distinction:

Plot armor: I cannot lose.
Faith: Loss would not necessarily mean the story was meaningless or finished.
Courage: Therefore I can move anyway.

That is considerably stronger, because it survives contact with reality. You don’t have to persuade yourself that you’re physically protected by the universe. You can retain uncertainty and still behave with conviction.

In our earlier language, it is almost a controlled prior: not “I know,” but “I am willing to allocate action on the assumption that continuing is worthwhile, then update from what happens.”

Luck applied, rather than luck presumed.